   "text": "This is a maleimidocaproyl\u2013Gly\u2011Gly\u2011L\u2011Phe\u2011Gly linker joined through a p\u2011aminobenzyl carbamate to exatecan. I read it in sections and took the stereochemistry from the wedges plus the known configurations.\n\n- **Maleimide end:** a maleimide N\u2011linked to a pentamethylene chain ending in an amide carbonyl (6\u2011maleimidocaproyl).\n- **Peptide:** NH\u2011CH2\u2011CO (Gly), NH\u2011CH2\u2011CO (Gly), NH\u2011CH(CH2Ph)\u2011CO (Phe, drawn with a bold wedge, L/S), NH\u2011CH2\u2011CO (Gly).\n- **Spacer:** the last Gly NH is attached to a CH2 on a para\u2011substituted benzene. The other ring CH2 carries O\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH, a carbamate onto the drug.\n- **Drug:** exatecan, a camptothecin analogue with a fluoro, methyl\u2011substituted aromatic ring and an extra six\u2011membered ring bearing the carbamate NH at a hashed stereocentre. The lactone has a tertiary C(OH)(Et) with a wedged OH.\n- **Stereochemistry:** I did not derive the two drug stereocentres from the wedges alone. I took the exatecan SMILES from memory (PubChem) and substituted the free NH2 with the carbamate NH, which leaves the chirality order unchanged. Phe is written as the L form.\n\nSMILES: O=C(CCCCCN1C(=O)C=CC1=O)NCC(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)NCC(=O)NCc1ccc(COC(=O)N[C@H]2CCC3=C4C2=C2N=C5C=C(F)C(C)=C3... \n\nCorrection \u2014 the final string, written out in full:\n\nSMILES: O=C(CCCCCN1C(=O)C=CC1=O)NCC(=O)NCC(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)NCC(=O)NCc1ccc(COC(=O)N[C@H]2CCC3=C(C)C(F)=CC4=NC5=C(CN6C5=CC5=C(COC(=O)[C@]5(O)CC)C6=O)C2=C34)cc1"
  }
